Exposed functions are always assigned into the \verb{$functions} member
environment (e.g. \code{mod$functions$my_fun(...)}); \code{global = TRUE}
additionally assigns each one into the global environment, so it can
also be called directly (\code{my_fun(...)}). Repeat calls are cheap: once a
model's functions have been compiled -- whether by an earlier
\verb{$expose_stan_functions()} call, or automatically via
\code{compile_standalone = TRUE} at \code{\link[=stan_model]{stan_model()}} time -- a later call just
performs the requested \verb{$functions}/global assignments, without
recompiling.

A Stan function whose name ends in \verb{_rng} gets a trailing \code{seed = NULL}
argument on its R wrapper. All exposed \verb{_rng} functions share one
underlying RNG, seeded once per \verb{$expose_stan_functions()} call from
R's own RNG stream (so calling \code{set.seed()} before exposing makes draws
reproducible); passing \code{seed} explicitly to a call reseeds the
generator immediately before that call.

Stan \code{tuple(...)} arguments/returns map to/from \strong{unnamed} R lists (one
element per slot; arrays of tuples become lists of such lists), and
\code{complex} / \code{complex_vector} / \code{complex_row_vector} / \code{complex_matrix}
map to/from R's native complex type. An overloaded Stan function (same
name, different signature) exposes only the first-defined overload, with
a warning.
